  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Prove that there is no rectangle with area $3\\text{ m}^2$ and perimeter $4\\text{ m}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No such rectangle exists.\n\nIts side lengths would satisfy $x^2-2x+3=0$, whose discriminant is $-8<0$.",
      "worked": "Let the side lengths be $x$ m and $y$ m. The area gives $xy=3$, and the perimeter gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+2y&=4\\\\x+y&=2\\\\y&=2-x.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into $xy=3$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x(2-x)&=3\\\\2x-x^2&=3\\\\x^2-2x+3&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-2)^2-4(1)(3)\\\\\\Delta&=4-12\\\\\\Delta&=-8<0.\\end{align*}$$\nThere are no real side lengths satisfying both conditions, so no such rectangle exists.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 116
  },
